<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ghent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gent, Gand</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ghent is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, at the meeting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> and Lys rivers. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the largest cities north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, rich from the cloth industry and often in revolt against the counts of Flanders. The Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> was born there in 1500, the Pacification of Ghent of 1576 united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> against Spanish rule, and King William I founded its university in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ghent.jpg" alt="Ghent" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michielverbeek · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gent,_de_Graslei_vanaf_de_Korenlei_met_oeg24758tm61%2B25159_IMG_0447_2021-08-13_18.37.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Land reclaimed from the sea and the coastal marshes supported large flocks of sheep, and from the eleventh century the towns of <b>Ghent</b>, Bruges, Ypres, Arras, Douai and Lille specialised in high-quality woollen cloth, increasingly made from imported English wool.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Coal from the Borinage, the Centre and the Liège basin fed ironworks such as John Cockerill’s at Seraing, the textile mills of <b>Ghent</b> and Verviers, and later glass, zinc and engineering industries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Skilled Protestant craftsmen from Antwerp, Bruges and <b>Ghent</b> moved north after Antwerp fell to Spain in 1585.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dutch-republic">Social Hierarchy of the Dutch Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>British and American negotiators signed a peace treaty at <b>Ghent</b>, restoring pre-war borders.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fought after the Treaty of <b>Ghent</b> was signed but before news arrived, it made Jackson a national hero.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-8">8 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V, born at <b>Ghent</b> in 1500, rounded off the territories by taking Frisia, Utrecht, Overijssel, Groningen and Guelders, forming the Seventeen Provinces.</blockquote><a href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
